// 图示Data包含参与者、steps骤、相关条目和说明。
// OSI上三层按功能展示；TCP/IP视图将这些条目合并到Application layer。
import {PROTOCOLS} from './content.js';
const rfc=n=>`https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc${n}.html`;
const extra={
 ftp:{name:'FTP',title:'File transfer',brief:'The control connection carries commands and replies; a separate data connection carries files or directory listings.',meta:'TCP 21 / separate data connection',source:rfc(959),topics:[{title:'Active and passive modes',text:'In active mode the server initiates the data connection, traditionally using TCP source port 20. In passive mode the client connects to the data port supplied by the server. Ports depend on the mode and negotiation.'},{title:'Security and related protocols',text:'Original FTP sends commands and data in cleartext. FTPS adds TLS protection to FTP; SFTP runs over SSH and uses a different protocol and connection model.'}]},
 imap:{name:'IMAP',title:'Server mailbox synchronization',brief:'Manage server-side mailboxes, messages and flags for synchronized access by multiple clients.',meta:'TCP 143 / implicit TLS 993',source:rfc(9051),topics:[{title:'Mailboxes and flags',text:'After authentication, the client selects a mailbox and reads UIDs, flags and message content. Read and delete operations update server state, which other clients can synchronize.'},{title:'Working with SMTP',text:'IMAP accesses and manages mailboxes; SMTP submits new mail. FETCH can retrieve a complete message, its headers or a specific part.'}]},
 pop3:{name:'POP3',title:'Mail retrieval',brief:'Connect to a mail server to list and retrieve messages; deleting server copies requires an explicit client operation.',meta:'TCP 110 / implicit TLS 995',source:rfc(1939),topics:[{title:'Three phases',text:'POP3 has authorization, transaction and update states. After authentication, commands include STAT, LIST and RETR. DELE marks messages; deletion is committed only after a normal QUIT from the transaction state. An abnormal disconnect does not commit deletion.'},{title:'Compared with IMAP',text:'POP3 focuses on retrieving mail; IMAP also organizes mailboxes and synchronizes flags. POP3 clients can retain server copies after downloading.'}]},
 websocket:{name:'WebSocket',title:'Bidirectional messaging',brief:'Once connected, either endpoint can send messages, supporting chat, notifications and other real-time interactions.',meta:'ws / wss',source:rfc(6455),topics:[{title:'Handshake and data frames',text:'The diagram uses an HTTP/1.1 Upgrade handshake followed by WebSocket frames. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 also support extended CONNECT mechanisms.'},{title:'Framing and security',text:'A message may span multiple frames. Ping/Pong checks connection status, and Close performs the closing handshake. Client masking only transforms frame payloads; wss provides TLS encryption.'}]},
 bgp:{name:'BGP',title:'Route advertisements and policy-based selection',brief:'Exchange network prefixes and path attributes, then choose routes according to policy.',meta:'TCP 179 · application-layer control protocol',source:rfc(4271),topics:[{title:'Paths and policy',text:'AS_PATH records the autonomous-system path and supports AS loop detection. Attributes such as LOCAL_PREF and AS_PATH participate in route selection; the result depends on policy and attribute comparisons.'},{title:'Protocol placement and purpose',text:'BGP runs over TCP, so this diagram places it in the TCP/IP application layer by its transport. It supplies routing information to the network layer: the control plane updates routes, and the data plane forwards packets using the forwarding table.'}]},
 icmpv6:{name:'ICMPv6 / NDP',title:'IPv6 diagnostics and Neighbor Discovery',brief:'ICMPv6 reports IPv6 errors and supports diagnostics. NDP uses ICMPv6 messages for neighbor resolution, router discovery and reachability detection.',meta:'IPv6 Next Header 58',source:rfc(4861),topics:[{title:'Neighbors and routers',text:'NS/NA support neighbor resolution; RS/RA support router discovery. RA can advertise a default router, prefixes and link parameters. Eligible prefixes can support Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC).'},{title:'Path MTU',text:'IPv6 routers do not fragment packets. Packet Too Big messages report the link MTU so the source can adjust packet sizes. Neighbor discovery and path MTU discovery require the relevant ICMPv6 messages to pass.'}]},
 ipsec:{name:'IPsec',title:'IP-layer security',brief:'Use security policies and security associations to protect IP traffic with confidentiality, integrity and data-origin authentication.',meta:'ESP / AH / IKE',source:rfc(4301),topics:[{title:'ESP, AH and IKE',text:'ESP can provide encryption, integrity and data-origin authentication, depending on the security association. AH provides integrity and data-origin authentication. IKE is a separate protocol that negotiates keys and security associations.'},{title:'Transport and tunnel modes',text:'ESP transport mode protects the IP payload. Tunnel mode protects the entire original IP packet and adds an outer IP header. This diagram uses ESP tunnel mode; its overhead reduces the inner data that fits within the same MTU.'}]},
 stp:{name:'STP / RSTP',title:'Layer-two loop prevention',brief:'Switches exchange Bridge Protocol Data Units (BPDUs) to establish a loop-free active topology while retaining physical redundancy.',meta:'IEEE 802.1Q bridging mechanisms',source:'https://1.ieee802.org/tsn/802-1q/',topics:[{title:'Root bridge and port roles',text:'STP selects the root bridge by bridge ID. Each non-root bridge selects a root port, and each segment a designated port. Other ports that need to block stop forwarding user frames.'},{title:'Changes in RSTP',text:'Rapid Spanning Tree Protocol (RSTP) adds roles such as alternate ports and accelerates convergence through proposal/agreement exchanges and edge-port behavior. Bridging prevents Layer 2 loops independently of IP TTL.'}]},
 ppp:{name:'PPP',title:'Point-to-point link encapsulation',brief:'Encapsulate network-layer packets on a point-to-point link and negotiate link parameters and network-layer configuration.',meta:'LCP / NCP',source:rfc(1661),topics:[{title:'Establishment order',text:'Link Control Protocol (LCP) first establishes and configures the link, followed by optional authentication. Network Control Protocols (NCPs) then configure network-layer protocols: IPCP for IPv4 and IPv6CP for IPv6.'},{title:'Use cases',text:'PPP serves point-to-point links and can run over Ethernet as PPPoE. PAP and CHAP provide optional authentication; encryption requires a suitable data-protection mechanism.'}]}
};
export const ATLAS={};
// step: [来源索引,目标索引,图中标题,解释]；flow的连线表示处理顺序。
function add(id,layer,kind,title,role,diagram,actors,steps,links,focus,note=''){
 const p=PROTOCOLS[id]||extra[id];
 ATLAS[id]={...p,id,layer,kind,title,role,diagram,actors,steps:steps.map(([from,to,label,text])=>({from,to,label,text})),links:links.map(([id,text])=>({id,text})),focus,note};
}
add('http','application','Protocol','HTTP · Requests and responses','Define request, response and resource-operation semantics; the underlying transport delivers the data.','sequence',['Browser','Web server'],[
 [0,1,'① GET /articles','The connection is established. The client requests a resource using a method, request target and header fields. HTTP/1.1 has a textual start line; HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 use different encodings.'],
 [1,1,'② Process request','The server finds a resource, checks permissions or runs application logic, then generates a status code and response content.'],
 [1,0,'③ 200 + response body','This example returns status 200, header fields and resource content. HTTP status describes request processing; a TCP acknowledgment number describes received byte progress.'],
 [0,0,'④ Parse and display','The browser interprets content using Content-Type and manages caching according to Cache-Control and related rules. Later requests may use a cache, conditional requests or an existing connection.']
], [['tls','HTTPS protects communication with TLS'],['tcp','HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 usually use TCP'],['quic','HTTP/3 uses QUIC'],['json','API bodies can use JSON']], 'Common fields include method, request target, status code, Content-Type and Cache-Control. GET has safe and idempotent semantics: it is intended for reading, and repeated execution has the same intended effect. Safe does not mean encrypted. POST has neither general guarantee. Idempotent operations can still return different content.','DNS lookup, connection establishment and the TLS handshake are omitted.');
add('dns','application','Protocol','DNS · Resource-record queries','Query addresses, mail routing and other information by name and record type.','sequence',['Client','Recursive resolver','Upstream DNS servers'],[
 [0,1,'① Query example.com A','The client asks a recursive resolver for an answer. This example uses A; AAAA, MX, CNAME and other types are also available.'],
 [1,1,'② Check cache','Valid cached records can answer directly. TTL limits how long a record may be cached.'],
 [1,2,'③ Cache miss: iterative lookup','The resolver follows referrals through root, top-level-domain and authoritative servers. This diagram combines those upstream servers into one participant.'],
 [2,1,'④ Return record and TTL','The authoritative server returns records, and the resolver caches the result according to the applicable rules. Nonexistent names or records can be negatively cached; some resolution failures also have caching rules.'],
 [1,0,'⑤ Return resolution result','After receiving an address, the client can connect to the application server. Port availability still requires an actual connection check.']
],[['udp','Traditional queries often use UDP 53'],['tcp','Traditional DNS also supports TCP 53'],['tls','DoT carries DNS over TLS'],['http','DoH carries DNS over HTTPS']],'A and AAAA records contain IPv4 and IPv6 addresses; CNAME records aliases; MX records mail-exchanger names and priorities. A truncated UDP response usually causes a TCP retry. DNS TTL measures time; IP TTL limits packet lifetime. They have different meanings.','The diagram omits DNSSEC and individual referrals. Encrypted transport protects the query connection; DNSSEC verifies DNS data origin and integrity.');
add('smtp','application','Protocol','SMTP · Submission, relay and delivery','Submit and relay mail; IMAP and POP3 provide mailbox access.','sequence',['Sending client','Sending server','Receiving server'],[
 [0,1,'① Submit mail','The client submits mail to a submission service, usually with user authentication. Common ports are 587 and implicit TLS 465; server-to-server relay uses TCP 25.'],
 [1,1,'② Look up recipient MX','The sending server queries MX records for the recipient domain and resolves the mail-exchanger addresses. When no MX records exist, SMTP also defines delivery using address records for the domain.'],
 [1,2,'③ MAIL / RCPT / DATA','MAIL FROM and RCPT TO specify envelope addresses; DATA transfers message headers and body. The From message header can differ from the envelope sender.'],
 [2,2,'④ Accept and deliver','After acceptance, delivery mechanisms store the message in a mailbox or relay it onward. Temporary failures can be queued for retry; final delivery failures may generate a bounce.']
],[['dns','MX records select relay destinations'],['tcp','A reliable byte stream carries commands and content'],['tls','STARTTLS or implicit TLS protects the link'],['imap','Recipient accesses and synchronizes a mailbox'],['pop3','Recipient retrieves mail']],'Commands include EHLO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA and QUIT. Reply codes 2xx indicate successful completion, 3xx request more input, 4xx indicate temporary failure, and 5xx permanent failure. Delivery after acceptance may happen asynchronously.');
add('dhcp','application','Protocol','DHCPv4 · Address leases and network configuration','Allocate address leases and supply options such as subnet mask, default gateway and DNS resolvers.','sequence',['Client','Server / relay'],[
 [0,1,'① DHCPDISCOVER','For initial configuration, the client sends discovery from 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255 using UDP source port 68 and destination port 67.'],
 [1,0,'② DHCPOFFER','The server offers an address, lease duration and configuration options. The client may receive several DHCPOFFER messages and select one.'],
 [0,1,'③ DHCPREQUEST','During initial selection, the client broadcasts a request identifying the selected server and requested address. Other servers withdraw their unselected offers.'],
 [1,0,'④ DHCPACK','The server acknowledges the lease. The client checks for address conflicts, applies the configuration and renews during the lease. A detected conflict can trigger DHCPDECLINE.']
],[['udp','Client 68, server 67'],['ipv4','Configure IPv4 addresses and routes'],['dns','Supplies resolver addresses']],'DORA is the common initial lease sequence; renewal usually sends DHCPREQUEST directly. Relays can forward messages to servers in other subnets. DHCPv6 uses UDP 546/547; IPv6 obtains its default route from Router Advertisements (RA).','The server node also represents an optional relay; no separate relay is drawn.');
add('ssh','application','Protocol','SSH · Host verification and a secure channel','Provide remote login, command execution and port forwarding.','sequence',['SSH client','SSH server'],[
 [0,1,'① Negotiate versions and algorithms','Exchange versions and algorithm lists over TCP, choosing key-exchange and protection algorithms.'],
 [1,0,'② Host identity and key exchange','The server proves its identity by signing with its host key. The client verifies a trusted host key, fingerprint or host certificate and completes key exchange. Identity verification helps detect server impersonation.'],
 [0,1,'③ Authenticate user','Password or public-key authentication runs inside the protected channel. User keys authenticate users; host keys authenticate servers.'],
 [0,1,'④ Open logical channels','Open terminal, SFTP or port-forwarding channels in the same connection. Multiple channels share the underlying TCP connection.']
],[['tcp','The server listens on TCP 22 by default'],['ftp','SFTP uses a different protocol from FTP and FTPS']],'SSH has its own key exchange and data protection. On first connection, verify the host fingerprint through a trusted channel. If a stored fingerprint changes, confirm whether the server key was replaced.');
add('ftp','application','Protocol','FTP · Control and data connections','The control connection carries commands and replies; the data connection carries files and directory listings.','sequence',['FTP client','FTP server'],[
 [0,1,'① Control connection and login','Connect to TCP 21 for authentication, directory and file commands. The control connection usually persists across file operations.'],
 [0,1,'② PASV / EPSV','In passive mode, the client requests a listening data port separate from control port 21.'],
 [1,0,'③ Return data port','PASV returns a server address and port; EPSV returns only a port, using the server address from the control connection.'],
 [0,1,'④ Connect and request a file','The client initiates the data connection and sends RETR over the control connection. The server returns file data over the data connection and a completion reply over the control connection.']
],[['tcp','Both control and data connections use TCP'],['tls','FTPS adds TLS protection to FTP'],['ssh','SFTP runs over SSH']],'USER, PASS, LIST, RETR and STOR travel over the control connection. Directory contents from LIST return over the data connection. Active mode has the server initiate the data connection, so firewalls and NAT must handle its ports and direction.','The diagram shows passive mode. The fourth arrow represents client connection establishment and the request; download data and intermediate replies are not drawn separately.');
add('imap','application','Protocol','IMAP · Mailbox access and flag synchronization','Access server-side mailboxes, retrieve content as needed and synchronize state.','sequence',['Mail client','Mailbox server'],[
 [0,1,'① Authenticate and SELECT','After authentication, the client selects a mailbox. The server reports message counts and state.'],
 [0,1,'② UID FETCH','Retrieve headers, bodies or individual parts by UID. A UID is unique within a particular mailbox UIDVALIDITY value; caches must record both.'],
 [1,0,'③ Return requested content','The server returns mail and flags. Clients may cache content for faster browsing or offline reading.'],
 [0,1,'④ STORE / synchronize flags','Clients update flags such as read state; other clients synchronize server state. New mail is still normally sent through SMTP.']
],[['smtp','New messages are submitted using SMTP'],['tls','Implicit TLS normally uses TCP 993'],['tcp','Carry commands and mail data'],['pop3','Another mail-retrieval protocol']],'\\Seen marks a message read; \\Deleted marks it for deletion. Operations such as EXPUNGE permanently remove marked messages. Clients share server-side mailbox state; synchronization depends on supported client and server features.');
add('pop3','application','Protocol','POP3 · Retrieve mail and commit deletion','List and download messages, and delete server copies when instructed by the client.','sequence',['Mail client','POP3 server'],[
 [0,1,'① Authenticate','After authentication, the client enters the transaction state. Implicit TLS or a STLS upgrade can protect the connection, depending on server configuration.'],
 [0,1,'② LIST / RETR','LIST returns message numbers and sizes; RETR returns the complete message, including headers and body. Clients normally store downloaded messages locally.'],
 [0,1,'③ Optional DELE','DELE marks a message for deletion; RSET can still cancel those marks. Unmarked messages remain on the server.'],
 [0,1,'④ QUIT commits updates','QUIT in the transaction state enters the update state, where the server processes deletions. An abnormal disconnect preserves previously marked messages.']
],[['smtp','Submit and relay mail'],['imap','Organize mailboxes and synchronize flags'],['tls','Implicit TLS normally uses TCP 995'],['tcp','The basic service uses TCP 110']],'Downloading and deleting are separate operations. DELE records marks during the transaction state; a normal QUIT enters the update state and processes them. An update failure may leave only some marked messages deleted.');
add('websocket','application','Protocol','WebSocket · Establish bidirectional messaging','After the handshake, either endpoint can send messages, each consisting of one or more frames.','sequence',['Browser','Real-time service'],[
 [0,1,'① HTTP Upgrade request','Classic HTTP/1.1 sends Upgrade, Connection and Sec-WebSocket-Key headers to request a protocol switch.'],
 [1,0,'② 101 Switching Protocols','The server accepts the upgrade and returns fields including Sec-WebSocket-Accept, which the client checks. Accept confirms the WebSocket handshake; user authentication remains an application responsibility.'],
 [0,1,'③ Client message frame','After establishment, send WebSocket frames. Client frames require masking; one logical message may span frames.'],
 [1,0,'④ Server push','The server can send messages at any time. Both endpoints can check connection status with Ping/Pong and close with a Close-frame handshake.']
],[['http','HTTP/1.1 uses an Upgrade handshake'],['tcp','Classic WebSocket uses TCP'],['tls','wss protects the connection with TLS'],['json','Message payloads may use JSON']],'Frame headers include FIN, opcode, MASK and payload length. Client frames must be masked; server frames must not be masked. Masking does not provide confidentiality.','This diagram uses HTTP/1.1 Upgrade and omits extended CONNECT over HTTP/2 and HTTP/3.');
add('bgp','application','Protocol','BGP · Neighbor sessions and policy-based routing','Advertise prefixes and path attributes to neighbors to supply routes for IP forwarding.','sequence',['AS 64500 router','AS 64501 router'],[
 [0,1,'① TCP connection and OPEN','Establish TCP with listening port 179, then exchange AS numbers, hold times and capabilities in OPEN.'],
 [1,0,'② KEEPALIVE confirmation','Both peers check OPEN and exchange KEEPALIVE messages to enter Established. With a nonzero hold time, subsequent KEEPALIVE or UPDATE messages keep the session alive.'],
 [1,0,'③ UPDATE prefixes and attributes','Announce reachable prefixes and attributes or withdraw routes. AS_PATH records AS paths and helps prevent loops.'],
 [0,0,'④ Apply policy and install routes','The receiver filters and selects routes according to import policy. Eligible routes enter the routing table, and the forwarding table supports actual packet forwarding.']
],[['tcp','The server listens on TCP 179'],['ipv4','Advertise IPv4 routes'],['ipv6','Multiprotocol extensions can advertise IPv6 routes'],['ospf','Provide routing within an autonomous system']],'BGP carries routing-control messages over TCP and is placed in the application layer here by its transport. It selects routes using path attributes and local policy; OSPF computes shortest paths using link costs.','This simplified eBGP exchange omits the full state machine and attribute comparison order.');
add('tls','presentation','Protocol / presentation function','TLS 1.3 · Key agreement and authentication','Negotiate keys, authenticate the peer, and protect application-data confidentiality and integrity.','sequence',['TLS client','TLS server'],[
 [0,1,'① ClientHello','The client sends supported versions, cipher suites and key shares. SNI and ALPN extensions can supply the server name and application-protocol list.'],
 [1,0,'② ServerHello','The server selects parameters and sends a key share. Both endpoints derive handshake traffic keys from the key exchange and handshake context.'],
 [1,0,'③ Certificate, signature and Finished','The server sends encrypted handshake messages. The client checks the certificate chain, target name, CertificateVerify signature and Finished to verify server identity and handshake integrity.'],
 [0,1,'④ Client Finished','The client sends Finished to confirm the handshake and prove possession of the handshake keys. If client certificates are requested, Certificate and CertificateVerify come first.'],
 [0,1,'⑤ Protected application data','Both endpoints protect data using derived application traffic keys and authenticated encryption. The certificate public key authenticates identity; symmetric keys encrypt application content.']
],[['http','HTTPS protects HTTP communication'],['tcp','TCP normally carries TLS records'],['quic','Use the TLS 1.3 handshake and QUIC packet protection']],'TLS provides security; TCP provides reliable delivery. Some metadata, such as endpoints and traffic volume, remains observable after encryption. Optional 0-RTT requires applications to consider replay risks.','This certificate-based full key-exchange handshake omits HelloRetryRequest and session resumption. The OSI view illustrates presentation functions; the TCP/IP view places it in the application layer.');
add('encoding','presentation','Encoding standard','UTF-8 · From code points to bytes','Encode Unicode code points into bytes using a common rule; the receiver decodes with the same rule.','flow',['Character U+7F51','Unicode code point','UTF-8 bytes','Protocol payload','Receiver decoding'],[
 [0,1,'① Identify code point','The example character has Unicode code point U+7F51. Characters, code points and visible glyphs do not always map one-to-one.'],
 [1,2,'② Encode bytes','UTF-8 encodes U+7F51 as E7 BD 91, three bytes. UTF-8 uses one to four bytes depending on the code point.'],
 [2,3,'③ Insert into message body','Protocols such as HTTP carry these bytes; application formats or protocol fields specify character encoding.'],
 [3,4,'④ Decode as UTF-8','The receiver recovers code points using UTF-8. An encoding mismatch can produce garbled text even when all transmitted bytes arrive correctly.']
],[['json','JSON exchanged between open systems must use UTF-8'],['http','Application formats specify body encoding']],'UTF-8 is a character encoding. Each code point occupies 1–4 bytes; one user-perceived character may contain several code points. Fields such as Content-Length count bytes; string length depends on the language and API.');
add('json','presentation','Data format','JSON · Serialization and parsing','Exchange structured data as text; applications define field meanings and validation rules.','flow',['Program data','JSON text','UTF-8 bytes','Transport message body','Parsing and validation'],[
 [0,1,'① Serialize','Represent objects as JSON text, for example {"id":7,"ok":true}. JSON supports objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans and null.'],
 [1,2,'② Encode as UTF-8','RFC 8259 requires UTF-8 for JSON text exchanged between open systems. Applications still need to agree on numeric ranges, date formats and field meanings.'],
 [2,3,'③ Carry in a protocol','JSON can appear in an HTTP body or WebSocket text message. When using TCP directly, applications must define message boundaries with lengths, delimiters or another mechanism.'],
 [3,4,'④ Parse and validate','The receiver parses JSON syntax, then checks field types, required fields and business rules. Syntactically valid input can still contain unacceptable application data.']
],[['encoding','Encode JSON text with UTF-8'],['http','Media type: application/json'],['websocket','JSON can serve as a message payload'],['rpc','JSON-RPC uses the JSON format']],'JSON has no native date or binary type and cannot preserve every language-specific object feature. Cross-language exchange needs agreed numeric ranges and precision, and should avoid duplicate object member names.');
add('rpc','session','Protocol family / invocation mechanism','RPC · Request correlation and call results','Represent remote operations as calls and handle arguments, results and failures.','sequence',['Caller','Remote service'],[
 [0,0,'① Serialize parameters','The call proxy encodes a method and arguments and establishes request correlation. ONC RPC and JSON-RPC can use message IDs; gRPC distinguishes calls through mechanisms such as HTTP/2 streams.'],
 [0,1,'② Send invocation','Send the request over the relevant application protocol and transport. Concurrent responses must match their requests; the correlation mechanism depends on the RPC protocol.'],
 [1,1,'③ Dispatch and execute','The server decodes parameters, selects a method and executes it. If execution succeeds but the response is lost, the client may still time out.'],
 [1,0,'④ Return result or error','Correlation delivers the response to the caller. Retries after timeouts require idempotency, deduplication or status queries to avoid duplicate execution.']
],[['json','JSON-RPC uses JSON serialization'],['http','Some RPC protocols use HTTP'],['tcp','Some RPC protocols run directly over TCP'],['session','Request correlation and interaction-state management']],'RPC includes many protocols and implementations. A response can be lost after successful remote execution, so reliable transport alone cannot guarantee exactly-once business operations.','This diagram illustrates functions related to session management; in actual TCP/IP stacks, RPC is an application-layer mechanism.');
add('session','session','Application mechanism','Application sessions · Login-state management','Associate user identity and state across requests and control session expiration.','sequence',['Browser','Application server'],[
 [0,1,'① Submit login credentials','Submit credentials over a protected connection for verification. Login and the TCP handshake operate at different levels.'],
 [1,0,'② Return session identifier','The server creates a session and returns an unpredictable identifier through Set-Cookie, with appropriate Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite attributes.'],
 [0,1,'③ Include identifier in later requests','A cookie links the request to session state, even when the TCP connection changes.'],
 [1,1,'④ Validate and expire','The server checks session validity and operation permissions on each request, and invalidates the session on logout or expiration.']
],[['http','Cookies associate requests'],['tls','Protects credentials and session IDs'],['rpc','Remote calls also need request correlation']],'OSI sessions, website login sessions and TCP connections are different concepts. This example uses server-side sessions; stateless tokens have different verification and revocation mechanisms. Checkpoints and resumable operations need separate application design.','Application session management is a class of mechanisms; this example uses website login.');
add('tcp','transport','Protocol','TCP · Byte acknowledgment and loss recovery','Provide an ordered byte stream and recover lost data using sequence numbers, acknowledgments and retransmission.','sequence',['Sender','Receiver'],[
 [0,1,'① seq=1001, 500 bytes','Assume an established connection sending bytes 1001–1500. TCP sequence numbers count bytes, not packets.'],
 [1,0,'② ACK，ack=1501','The receiver cumulatively acknowledges through 1500 and expects 1501. The ACK flag and acknowledgment-number field are distinct.'],
 [0,1,'③ seq=1501 lost','A segment carrying bytes 1501–2000 is sent but lost in transit.'],
 [0,1,'④ Retransmit missing data','The sender resends data after a retransmission timeout or other loss detection. This diagram retransmits the same byte range; actual retransmission may use different segment boundaries.'],
 [1,0,'⑤ ACK，ack=2001','Once contiguous bytes are complete, the cumulative acknowledgment advances to 2001 and the application reads them in order. TCP acknowledgments indicate transport reception; application processing needs separate confirmation.']
],[['ipv4','IP delivers packets'],['http','HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 usually use TCP'],['tls','TLS provides security']],'Sending is limited by receive window rwnd, congestion window cwnd and bytes in flight. TCP is a byte stream, so applications must define message boundaries. SYN and FIN each consume one sequence number; pure ACK does not. Unrecoverable connection failures are still reported to the application.','This diagram begins with an established connection and focuses on loss recovery; the TCP/UDP lab covers establishment and closing.');
add('udp','transport','Protocol','UDP · Delivery of independent datagrams','Use ports to distinguish endpoints and preserve each datagram boundary.','sequence',['Sender','Receiver'],[
 [0,1,'① Datagram 1','Each submission forms an independent UDP datagram. Length includes the 8-byte header and data.'],
 [0,1,'② Datagram 2 lost','IP paths can lose packets. UDP supplies no acknowledgments, timeout retransmission or ordering itself.'],
 [0,1,'③ Datagram 3 arrives','Datagram 3 arrives while datagram 2 is missing. Applications needing loss or reordering detection can include sequence information in the payload.'],
 [1,1,'④ Application chooses recovery','The application handles gaps, discards stale data or implements recovery. A UDP checksum detects errors but does not provide reliable delivery.']
],[['dns','Traditional DNS often uses UDP'],['quic','Implement reliable, secure transport over UDP'],['ipv4','IP provides best-effort delivery']],'The UDP header contains source port, destination port, length and checksum, each 16 bits. A zero IPv4 UDP checksum means omitted checking. IPv6 normally requires a UDP checksum, with restricted exceptions for certain tunnels. Numbers in this diagram are application data; UDP headers have no datagram sequence number.','This example shows loss; UDP traffic can also arrive out of order or duplicated.');
add('quic','transport','Protocol','QUIC · Secure handshake and multiple streams','Implement stream multiplexing, loss recovery, congestion control and a TLS 1.3 handshake over UDP.','sequence',['QUIC client','QUIC server'],[
 [0,1,'① Initial / ClientHello','UDP carries initial QUIC packets and TLS handshake data. Packet numbers support recovery; stream offsets support data reassembly.'],
 [1,0,'② Handshake and authentication','The server returns TLS handshake information; the client verifies server identity, and both derive application-data keys. QUIC carries the TLS handshake and protects data using QUIC packet protection.'],
 [0,1,'③ Multiplex STREAM data','Streams within a connection deliver ordered bytes independently. Missing data in one stream does not prevent others from delivering received contiguous bytes.'],
 [1,0,'④ ACK received packet ranges','ACK frames identify received packet ranges. After detecting loss, the sender resends still-needed data in new packets with new packet numbers.'],
 [0,1,'⑤ Path change and validation','After handshake confirmation, a client may switch addresses when migration is permitted. Connection IDs identify the connection; path validation checks reachability over the new path.']
],[['udp','Carry QUIC packets'],['tls','TLS 1.3 provides the security handshake'],['http','HTTP/3 uses QUIC']],'QUIC handles acknowledgments, recovery, congestion control and flow control. Independent streams reduce transport-level head-of-line blocking across streams, but each stream still waits for missing bytes, and applications can have cross-stream dependencies. Migration depends on negotiated parameters and connection IDs.');
add('ipv4','network','Protocol','IPv4 · Hop-by-hop routing and re-encapsulation','Look up the destination route, select a next hop and provide best-effort packet delivery.','sequence',['Host A','Router','Host B'],[
 [0,0,'① Look up route and next hop','The host looks up the destination route. A connected route usually selects the destination itself; a remote route usually selects a gateway. Ethernet then resolves that next-hop MAC address with ARP.'],
 [0,1,'② Deliver frame to next hop','IP endpoints remain A/B; this frame targets the gateway MAC. Ethernet handles one hop, IP addressing spans networks.'],
 [1,1,'③ Longest-prefix match','The router removes the current link encapsulation and applies longest-prefix matching. Forwarding decrements TTL and updates the IPv4 header checksum; an expired TTL causes a drop.'],
 [1,2,'④ Re-encapsulate for next hop','The router re-encapsulates with its outgoing-interface source MAC and the next-hop destination MAC. Ordinary forwarding preserves source and destination IPs. Oversized packets are fragmented or discarded with error feedback according to MTU and DF.'],
 [2,2,'⑤ Destination processes payload','The destination uses the protocol number to deliver to TCP, UDP or ICMP. Fragments are reassembled first where applicable.']
],[['arp','Resolves IPv4 next-hop MAC'],['ethernet','Deliver frames across each Ethernet link'],['icmp','Report some forwarding errors'],['ospf','Supply routing information']],'IPv4 has 32-bit addresses and a 20-byte base header. Longest-prefix matching chooses the most specific matching route; default /0 applies when no more specific route matches. NAT rewrites addresses; NAPT can also rewrite ports.','This diagram assumes Ethernet at every hop and omits NAT, tunnels and policy routing.');
add('ipv6','network','Protocol','IPv6 · Neighbor resolution and path MTU','Use 128-bit addresses, a 40-byte base header and extension headers.','sequence',['IPv6 host A','IPv6 router','IPv6 host B'],[
 [0,0,'① Route and resolve neighbor','Select a next hop and resolve its link address with NDP. IPv6 does not use IPv4 ARP.'],
 [0,1,'② Send IPv6 packet','The base header includes source address, destination address, Next Header and Hop Limit. IPv6 has no base-header checksum.'],
 [1,1,'③ Check Hop Limit and MTU','Forwarding decrements Hop Limit; expiration causes a drop and a Time Exceeded response. A packet exceeding the outgoing-link MTU is also dropped, with the MTU reported through ICMPv6 Packet Too Big.'],
 [1,2,'④ Forward packets that fit the MTU','Eligible packets continue to the next hop. The source adjusts sizes for the path MTU and can use a Fragment extension header if needed; the destination performs reassembly.']
],[['icmpv6','Provide neighbor discovery and MTU feedback'],['tcp','Provide reliable transport over IPv6'],['ethernet','Can carry IPv6 packets']],'Next Header identifies the next extension header or upper-layer protocol. IPv6 defines unicast, multicast and anycast, but no broadcast. NDP can use multicast or unicast. IPv6 routers do not fragment; security still requires separate configuration.','Step three includes a discard branch; only eligible packets proceed to step four.');
add('icmp','network','Protocol','ICMP · Echo and error feedback','Report some IP-processing errors and provide diagnostic messages such as Echo.','sequence',['Probing host','Router / target'],[
 [0,1,'① Send probe','ping commonly sends ICMP Echo Request. traceroute may use UDP, TCP or other probes with increasing TTL.'],
 [1,1,'② Process the condition','A target can echo; routers may report expired TTL or unreachable destinations according to protocol and policy.'],
 [1,0,'③ Echo Reply / Time Exceeded','The destination may return an Echo Reply; intermediate routers may return Time Exceeded. ICMP errors include part of the original packet so the sender can associate the response with a probe.'],
 [0,0,'④ Match and analyze','The tool matches feedback and calculates round-trip times or records hops. Filtering, rate limiting and path faults can all cause missing replies; compare with actual connection behavior.']
],[['ipv4','Carry ICMPv4'],['icmpv6','IPv6 uses ICMPv6'],['tcp','Test TCP services with a separate connection']],'ICMP has type, code and checksum fields, with no TCP or UDP ports. It reports errors but does not retransmit IP packets. A successful Echo probe only establishes that a reply arrived; service health needs additional checks.');
add('ospf','network','Protocol','OSPF · Link-state synchronization and SPF','Exchange link state within an autonomous system and calculate paths using link costs.','sequence',['Router R1','Neighbors / area routers'],[
 [0,1,'① Discover neighbors with Hello','Routers exchange Hello messages and check key parameters to form neighbor relationships. Full adjacency also depends on link type and roles such as DR/BDR.'],
 [0,1,'② Synchronize link-state database','Adjacent routers synchronize Link State Advertisements (LSAs) through Database Description, Link State Request and Link State Update packets. After convergence, routers in the same area have consistent databases.'],
 [1,0,'③ Flood changed LSAs','Link changes trigger LSA updates. Sequence numbers, LSA age and acknowledgments distinguish newer information and propagate updates reliably.'],
 [0,0,'④ Run SPF and derive routes','Each router computes a shortest-path tree rooted at itself and generates routes; routing and forwarding tables support packet delivery.']
],[['ipv4','OSPFv2 uses IP protocol number 89'],['bgp','Advertise routes according to policy']],'OSPF operates in the control plane; IP forwarding operates in the data plane. Areas and the backbone organize the topology. DR/BDR roles reduce adjacency and flooding overhead on broadcast and similar networks.','This diagram combines several area routers and shows only the core single-area process.');
add('icmpv6','network','Protocol family','ICMPv6 / NDP · Router discovery and neighbor resolution','Use ICMPv6 messages to discover routers, resolve next-hop link addresses and check reachability.','sequence',['IPv6 host A','Neighbor B / router'],[
 [0,1,'① RS: request router information','The host sends Router Solicitation (RS) to obtain router information for the local link.'],
 [1,0,'② RA: advertise parameters','The router sends Router Advertisement (RA) with prefixes and link parameters. Router Lifetime indicates default-router eligibility. Routers also send periodic RAs.'],
 [0,1,'③ NS: resolve a neighbor','When the neighbor cache lacks a mapping, the host sends Neighbor Solicitation (NS) to the next-hop solicited-node multicast address.'],
 [1,0,'④ NA: return neighbor information','The neighbor sends Neighbor Advertisement (NA), which can include a link-layer address option. The host updates its neighbor cache and sends frames to the next hop.'],
 [0,0,'⑤ Maintain neighbor reachability','Neighbor Unreachability Detection (NUD) maintains neighbor states and sends unicast NS probes when needed. Packet Too Big is a separate ICMPv6 message type providing path MTU feedback.']
],[['ipv6','Support IPv6 local-link communication'],['arp','IPv4 resolves next hops with ARP'],['ethernet','Resolve a next-hop Ethernet MAC address']],'NDP also provides prefix discovery and redirects. SLAAC uses eligible RA prefixes; Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) uses NS/NA. Each has additional configuration rules.','Neighbors and routers share one diagram participant. Address resolution, router discovery and DAD can use different message destination addresses.');
add('ipsec','network','Protocol family','IPsec · ESP tunnel encapsulation','Protect an inner IP packet through a security association; outer IP addresses route the tunnel traffic.','flow',['Security association','Original IP packet','ESP protection','Outer IP forwarding','Validate and decapsulate'],[
 [0,1,'① Establish security associations','IKE negotiates algorithms, keys and parameters to establish ESP Security Associations (SAs). ESP and AH SAs are unidirectional, so bidirectional traffic requires separate SAs; the IKE SA itself is bidirectional.'],
 [1,2,'② Protect the original packet','ESP tunnel mode carries the complete original IP packet as inner payload. This example encrypts with integrity protection and maintains sequence numbers for replay prevention.'],
 [2,3,'③ Add an outer IP header','Outer addresses identify tunnel endpoints. Intermediate routers forward using the outer destination; the inner packet is protected while outer routing information remains visible.'],
 [3,4,'④ Verify and decapsulate','The peer selects an inbound SA using SPI and related information, then authenticates, checks replay and decrypts. Successful verification permits decapsulation and processing of the inner packet.']
],[['ipv4','Can protect IPv4 packets'],['ipv6','Can protect IPv6 packets'],['udp','NAT traversal can encapsulate ESP in UDP'],['tls','Provide security at a different layer']],'ESP can encrypt and authenticate; AH provides integrity and data-origin authentication. Tunnel overhead requires considering path MTU, NAT traversal and security policies.','This diagram uses encrypted and authenticated ESP tunnel mode and omits IKE exchanges. Other SAs can select different algorithms and protection combinations.');
add('ethernet','datalink','Link technology','Ethernet · MAC learning and frame forwarding','Deliver frames by MAC address within a local Layer 2 network.','sequence',['Host A','Switch','Host B'],[
 [0,1,'① Frame arrives at ingress','Frames contain destination/source MAC, type and payload. The switch learns the source port and VLAN from the source MAC.'],
 [1,1,'② Look up destination MAC','A destination on another port is forwarded there; one on the incoming port is filtered. Unknown unicasts and broadcasts normally flood within the VLAN, excluding the incoming port.'],
 [1,2,'③ Forward to target port','The switch forwards to the port for B, delivering within the same Layer 2 domain. Communication across IP subnets normally requires routing.'],
 [2,2,'④ Validate and deliver upward','The receiving interface checks FCS and delivers the payload to IPv4, ARP, IPv6 or another protocol according to EtherType. FCS detects errors; Ethernet itself usually does not retransmit damaged frames.']
],[['arp','Resolves IPv4 next-hop MAC'],['vlan','Separates broadcast domains'],['stp','Prevent loops on redundant switched links'],['phy','Convert frame data to signals']],'A typical Ethernet II header is 14 bytes and FCS is 4 bytes. The usual 1500-byte limit refers to payload and the corresponding IP MTU. Preamble and interframe gap add separate wire overhead.','This diagram uses switched full-duplex Ethernet. CSMA/CD applies to shared half-duplex Ethernet; full-duplex links do not use collision detection.');
add('wifi','datalink','Wireless link technology','Wi-Fi · Channel contention and link acknowledgments','Use 802.11 frames and medium-access mechanisms to carry data over a wireless link.','sequence',['Wireless station','Access point'],[
 [0,0,'① Sense the channel and back off','Contention-based access senses the channel and follows backoff rules after required interframe intervals. The counter decreases while idle and pauses while busy. Wireless access uses collision avoidance.'],
 [0,1,'② Send 802.11 data frame','Send after gaining access. Association, security authentication and optional RTS/CTS are omitted.'],
 [1,0,'③ Link ACK','Many unicast transmissions use ACK or Block Ack. Missing required acknowledgments can cause retries; broadcasts and multicasts normally have no per-frame acknowledgment.'],
 [1,1,'④ Bridge to another link','An AP can bridge wireless traffic to wired links. Wireless ACKs and end-to-end TCP ACKs operate at different layers.']
],[['ethernet','APs often bridge to wired Ethernet'],['phy','The wireless PHY encodes and modulates signals'],['tcp','TCP provides end-to-end acknowledgments']],'IEEE 802.11 includes both MAC and PHY standards. CSMA/CA serves contention-based access; modern Wi-Fi also supports scheduled transmission. RTS/CTS is conditional. Link and TCP acknowledgments address local-link and end-to-end delivery respectively.','This common unicast contention sequence omits association, authentication and other access methods.');
add('arp','datalink','Protocol / layer boundary','ARP · Resolve a next-hop MAC address','Resolve a selected next-hop IPv4 address to a link-layer address on the local link.','sequence',['Host A','Local link / gateway'],[
 [0,0,'① Check route and ARP cache','The host first checks its IPv4 route, then its next-hop ARP cache. B is remote in this example, so the gateway is the next hop and its MAC is resolved.'],
 [0,1,'② Broadcast ARP Request','A asks for the gateway MAC within the local broadcast domain, using Ethernet destination ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff. Ethernet carries the ARP request directly.'],
 [1,0,'③ ARP Reply','The gateway normally unicasts an ARP reply. ARP contains sender and target protocol and hardware addresses; A obtains the gateway IP-to-MAC mapping.'],
 [0,1,'④ Cache and send data frame','A caches the mapping and wraps the IP packet for B in a frame targeting the gateway MAC. The cache can expire or update.']
],[['ipv4','Select a route before resolving the next hop'],['ethernet','Carry ARP messages directly'],['icmpv6','IPv6 resolves neighbors with NDP']],'ARP connects IP addressing with link delivery and is placed in the link layer here. ARP broadcasts remain in the local broadcast domain; ordinary routers do not forward them to other domains.','Proxy ARP and address-conflict detection are omitted.');
add('vlan','datalink','Link tagging and mechanism','802.1Q VLAN · Tags and broadcast domains','Divide Layer 2 broadcast domains through port membership and VLAN tags.','sequence',['VLAN 10 host','Switch S1','Switch S2'],[
 [0,1,'① Receive on access port','A typical access port receives an untagged endpoint frame and assigns it to VLAN 10 according to configuration.'],
 [1,2,'② Carry VLAN tag on trunk','The trunk permits VLAN 10 and adds an 802.1Q tag to this frame. The 4-byte tag includes PCP priority, Drop Eligible Indicator (DEI) and VLAN ID.'],
 [2,2,'③ Learn and forward within VLAN','The switch forwards using VLAN and MAC-table entries; broadcasts reach only eligible ports in the same VLAN.'],
 [2,2,'④ Send to an access port','S2 removes the tag when sending to a VLAN 10 access port. Reaching VLAN 20 requires Layer 3 forwarding and applicable access rules.']
],[['ethernet','Insert an 802.1Q tag into an Ethernet frame'],['stp','Prevent switched loops within VLANs'],['ipv4','Inter-VLAN traffic usually requires routing']],'VLANs divide Layer 2 broadcast domains; IP subnets describe Layer 3 prefixes. They are often planned together. Trunks carry multiple VLANs; native-VLAN tagging depends on configuration.','This diagram uses a tagged inter-switch link; the endpoint beyond S2 is not drawn separately.');
add('stp','datalink','Bridge control protocol','STP / RSTP · Root bridge and loop-free topology','Exchange BPDUs to select a loop-free forwarding topology and prevent persistent frame loops.','flow',['Exchange BPDUs','Select root bridge','Compute port roles','Restrict redundant forwarding','Reconverge after changes'],[
 [0,1,'① Compare bridge information','Switches compare bridge information from BPDUs; classic STP selects the lowest bridge ID as root.'],
 [1,2,'② Choose path to root','Non-root bridges select root ports using root-path cost and related information; each segment selects a designated port. Ties also compare bridge IDs and port IDs.'],
 [2,3,'③ Block redundant paths','Roles and states restrict user-frame forwarding for a loop-free active topology while keeping physical redundancy.'],
 [3,4,'④ Handle link changes','A failure or topology change triggers convergence and may activate a previously non-forwarding port. RSTP accelerates switching with proposal/agreement and alternate-port mechanisms.']
],[['ethernet','Control Layer 2 switching loops'],['vlan','Coordinate spanning tree with VLAN topology']],'Layer 2 switching does not decrement IP TTL. Frames can loop indefinitely, causing broadcast storms and MAC-table instability. Spanning tree controls the active topology; switches still forward individual frames using their MAC tables.','This algorithm overview omits the specific port-state transitions of STP and RSTP.');
add('ppp','datalink','Protocol family','PPP · Link negotiation and network-layer configuration','Provide encapsulation and negotiation for point-to-point connections carrying different network-layer protocols.','sequence',['Endpoint A','Endpoint B'],[
 [0,1,'① Negotiate link with LCP','Peers negotiate link options through Configure-Request, Configure-Ack, Configure-Nak and Configure-Reject messages.'],
 [1,0,'② Authenticate if required','If authentication is negotiated, PAP or CHAP verifies identity. Payload encryption requires separate configuration.'],
 [0,1,'③ Configure the network layer with NCP','The relevant NCP negotiates network-layer parameters. IPCP can negotiate IPv4 addresses; IPv6CP negotiates interface identifiers. IPv6 addresses and prefixes require additional IPv6 mechanisms.'],
 [0,1,'④ Encapsulate network packets','The PPP Protocol field identifies the payload type. HDLC-like framing uses FCS to detect frame errors; other underlying encapsulations can use different framing and checks.']
],[['ipv4','IPCP configuration and IPv4 packet carriage'],['ipv6','IPv6CP configuration and IPv6 packet carriage'],['phy','Uses underlying link transmission']],'PPP connects two endpoints without Ethernet-style multipoint MAC addressing. The PPPoE header and PPP Protocol field normally total 8 bytes, reducing a typical 1500-byte Ethernet MTU to a 1492-byte IP MTU. Supported extensions allow other values.');
add('phy','physical','Physical standard / function','PHY · Encoding, transmission and synchronization','Convert digital information into transmissible signals and recover it at the receiver.','flow',['Upper-layer digital information','Encoding and boundary markers','Modulation / medium signals','Receive and synchronize','Recovered bits'],[
 [0,1,'① Receive bit sequence','The physical layer receives digital information from the link interface. Interface and coding granularity depend on the standard.'],
 [1,2,'② Encode and transmit','Line coding, scrambling or modulation converts information to signals. Ethernet and wireless PHYs use different schemes.'],
 [2,3,'③ Propagate through the medium','Electrical signals, light or radio waves encounter attenuation, noise and interference in the medium. Link bit rate and signal propagation speed measure different properties.'],
 [3,4,'④ Synchronize and decode','The receiver recovers timing or symbols and reconstructs data. Some PHYs use Forward Error Correction (FEC); link-layer checks can detect errors that remain uncorrected.']
],[['medium','Provide a signal-propagation environment'],['ethernet','Include different wired PHYs'],['wifi','Include corresponding wireless PHYs']],'1000BASE-T, 10GBASE-SR and 802.11 PHYs use different technologies. Bit rate counts bits per second; baud rate counts symbols per second. Their relationship depends on modulation and coding. Autonegotiation and FEC support depend on the specific standard.','This diagram summarizes shared functions; actual PHY interfaces, coding and signal-processing steps vary.');
add('medium','physical','Media and physical concepts','Transmission media · Transmission and propagation delay','Twisted pair, fiber and wireless provide different propagation environments, affecting distance, loss and capacity.','flow',['1500 bytes of data','Put bits on link','Propagate 1000km','Receive signals','Link / higher layers'],[
 [0,1,'① Transmission delay L / R','1500 bytes equal 12000 bits, requiring 0.12 ms to transmit at 100 Mb/s. This excludes encapsulation and line-coding overhead.'],
 [1,2,'② Propagation delay d / v','Over 1000 km at 2 × 10⁸ m/s, one-way propagation takes 5 ms. A higher bit rate reduces transmission delay; propagation delay stays the same for the same distance and medium.'],
 [2,3,'③ Signals and loss','The receiver handles attenuation, noise and interference. Fiber also has loss and dispersion, and its range depends on optical modules and link specifications.'],
 [3,4,'④ Add other delays','Processing, queuing, handshakes and retransmissions add end-to-end delay. Under these assumptions, the final bit arrives after 0.12 + 5 = 5.12 ms.']
],[['phy','Map data to signals in the medium'],['ethernet','Can use copper and fiber'],['wifi','Use a shared wireless medium']],'Spectrum bandwidth is measured in Hz and link capacity in bit/s, so the everyday term bandwidth needs a unit. Throughput is the actual transfer rate; goodput excludes protocol overhead. Delay, capacity, error rate and distance measure different properties.','This calculation uses one hypothetical link. Transmission and propagation overlap in time; end-to-end networks normally contain multiple links and devices.');

export const PROTOCOL_LAYER_KEYS={osi:['application','presentation','session','transport','network','datalink','physical'],tcpip:['tcp-application','tcp-transport','internet','interface']};
export function protocolIds(model,layer){const groups={ 'tcp-application':['application','presentation','session'],'tcp-transport':['transport'],internet:['network'],interface:['datalink','physical']};return Object.keys(ATLAS).filter(id=>(groups[layer]||[layer]).includes(ATLAS[id].layer));}
export const LAYER_GUIDES={
 application:'Application protocols define interactions for mail, web resources, files and other services. BGP carries routing-control messages over TCP and is placed here by its transport.',
 presentation:'The OSI presentation layer handles data representation. These examples illustrate character encoding, serialization and data protection; the TCP/IP view includes them in its application layer.',
 session:'The OSI session layer organizes and synchronizes sessions. RPC correlation and website login illustrate related functions implemented by application protocols or frameworks in actual TCP/IP stacks.',
 transport:'Transport services use ports to distinguish endpoints. TCP provides reliable byte streams, UDP provides datagrams, and QUIC implements secure multistream transport over UDP.',
 network:'IP addresses and forwards packets, ICMP reports errors, and OSPF supplies routing-control information. The control plane computes routes; the data plane forwards packets.',
 datalink:'Frame formats, medium access and bridging provide local delivery. ARP connects IP addressing with link-address resolution. Ethernet and Wi-Fi standards also include physical-layer specifications.',
 physical:'The physical layer specifies signals, coding and synchronization. Physical-layer standards and media are listed together here to explain transmission across real links.',
 'tcp-application':'The application layer includes service interactions, representation, security and session functions. Application protocols, libraries and frameworks implement these together in real systems.',
 'tcp-transport':'The transport layer delivers between endpoints. TCP uses byte streams, UDP datagrams, and QUIC multiple streams over UDP.',
 internet:'The internet layer handles addressing and forwarding across networks, including error feedback, IP-layer security and relevant routing-control mechanisms.',
 interface:'The network interface layer includes local-link delivery and physical transmission: framing, medium access, bridging, signals and media.'
};
